.TH PLEXDO 1 "2026-11-10" "plexdo 3.0.0" "User Commands"
.
.SH NAME
plexdo \- command\-line interface for Plex Media Server
.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B plexdo
.RI [ global-options ]
.I command
.RI [ command-options ]
.RI [ arguments ...]
.br
.B plexdo
.RI [ global-options ]
.I command
.RI [ command-options ]
.RI [ arguments ...]
.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B plexdo
builds and copies playlists, exports libraries to M3U or a static HTML photo
gallery, synchronises watched state between users, streams media to standard
output, and reports on server activity.
.PP
.B plexdo
is an alias for the same program; prefer it on Windows, where a dot in a
command name interacts awkwardly with
.BR PATHEXT .
.PP
Wherever a
.I user
is required you may give the numeric user ID or the user\(aqs title, and
wherever a
.I library
is required you may give the numeric library ID or its title. User ID
.B 0
always means the admin account. Titles are matched exactly first, then
case\-insensitively. If a value is both one entry\(aqs ID and another\(aqs title the
ID wins and a warning names the entry that was skipped; if two entries share a
title the command aborts and asks for the numeric ID.
.PP
Commands that take an existing playlist accept its name or its ratingKey
interchangeably.
.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
These may be given either before or after the command name.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-format " \fIFORMAT\fR"
Output format: \fBtable\fR (default), \fBjson\fR, \fByaml\fR, \fBcsv\fR, or
\fBclixml\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Shorthand for \fB\-\-format json\fR.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Print high\-level progress to standard error.
.TP
.B \-\-debug
Print detailed internal logs to standard error.
.TP
.BI \-\-debug\-log " PATH"
Append the detailed internal logs to
.I PATH
instead, created with mode 0600, and profile the run: every line starts with
the time it was written in nanoseconds since the Unix epoch, and beside the
debug logs the file records each phase's run time, every HTTP request with its
headers, status, sizes, and wait, CPU and memory use every five seconds, and
cProfile's table of function run times. Secrets are redacted, there and on
standard error. Standard error shows what it would without
.BR \-\-debug .
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Show what would happen without mutating Plex.
.TP
.BR \-W ", " \-\-wide
Do not shrink table columns to the terminal width. By default the widest
column is truncated, with an ellipsis, so that a row fits on one line, and the
columns narrowed are reported at
.B \-\-verbose
level. Redirected output is never truncated, so piping to a file or to
.BR grep (1)
always carries the whole value.
.TP
.BI \-\-throttle " SECONDS"
Seconds to wait between requests in operations that query once per item, when
there are more than 50 of them. Defaults to 0.25; pass 0 to disable. Walking
every season of a large library, or writing watched state item by item, is
otherwise enough traffic to slow a server down or trip its rate limiting.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the installed version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print help and exit. Every command accepts this.
.PP
All logging goes to standard error, or to the
.B \-\-debug\-log
file, so machine\-readable output on standard output is always safe to pipe.
.
.SH OUTPUT FORMATS
.TP
.B table
Box\-drawn and width\-aligned, accounting for double\-width characters. Falls
back to ASCII box characters when the console encoding cannot represent the
Unicode ones.
.TP
.B json
Non\-ASCII is escaped, so it prints on any console.
.TP
.B yaml
Strings are always quoted, so a title such as \fBNO\fR or \fB1.10\fR survives
as a string rather than being reinterpreted as a boolean or a float.
.TP
.B csv
Header row plus one row per record, with newline line endings.
.TP
.B clixml
PowerShell CLIXML with typed properties, for \fBImport\-Clixml\fR.
.
.SH COMMANDS
The commands are listed here as
.B plexdo \-\-help
lists them: grouped by what they act on, and by name within each group.
Most are named for what they act on, then what they do to it.
.SS Libraries and titles
.TP
.BI album\-list " ratingKey"
Show one music or photo album: the album, with its played state and
progress, then a row per track, photo, or clip, with its state and, when in
progress, its resume point, length, and percentage, as
.BR "3:20 / 9:46 (34.1%)" .
JSON and YAML carry the same record
.B albums\-list
gives for each album; CSV and CLIXML, which cannot nest, carry one row per
item. Albums nested in a photo album are left out, and counted. Anything but
an album is refused.
.TP
.BI albums\-list " library"
List the albums in a music or photo library, nested photo albums included.
Table output shows each album\(aqs rating key, artist (music only), title,
year, item count, and how many items you have played. A machine\-readable
format adds each album\(aqs
.B state
.RB ( played ", " "in progress" ", or " unplayed ),
its
.B progress
as a percentage of its running time when in progress, and its
.B contents
in order: every track, photo, or clip, with its own state, view count, last
viewed time,
.BR viewOffset ,
.BR progress ,
and files. A music library\(aqs table costs one request; its other formats,
and any photo library, which must be opened to find nested albums, cost one
per album. Photos have no played state, so theirs is null. Other library
types are refused.
.TP
.B libraries\-list
List every library with its ID, type, and title.
.TP
.BI library\-list " library " [ \-\-album " ALBUM" ]
List the titles in a library. Table output shows the rating key, title,
release date, rating, and studio; a photo library lists its photos, those in
albums inside albums included. A machine\-readable format carries every
field the server returned for each item, and for a show library nests each
show\(aqs episodes under a
.B seasons
object keyed by season name. Nested objects such as media and their parts are
expanded, so a file name is at
.BR media[].parts[].file ,
and the paths are repeated in a top\-level
.B files
list for convenience. Note that
.B sourceURI
is not a file name: it is the remote server URI of an item in someone else\(aqs
playlist, and is null for anything on your own server. \fB\-\-album\fR restricts a photo library to one
album and is ignored elsewhere.
.
.TP
.BI missing\-find " SHOW " [ \-l " LIBRARY" "] [" \-A "] [" \-s " SEASONS" "] [" \-\-include\-specials ]
Report the episode numbers missing between the start of a season and its
highest episode.
.I SHOW
is a show title or ratingKey.
.RS
.PP
A season that simply stops early is not reported, since an unaired episode
cannot be told from a missing one.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-library " \fILIBRARY\fR"
Look only in this library, by ID or title. Narrows the search for
.IR SHOW ,
or the sweep for
.BR \-A .
.TP
.BR \-A ", " \-\-all
Check every show rather than one named show.
.I SHOW
must be omitted.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-season " \fISEASONS\fR"
One season number, or several separated by commas, as in
.BR 1,3,5 .
Applies to a single show. Naming season 0 includes the specials.
.TP
.B \-\-include\-specials
Also check season 0 when no season is named. Specials are numbered
irregularly, so this is off by default.
.RE
.
.TP
.BI search " user query " [ \-\-media\-type " TYPE" "] [" \-\-library\-id " LIBRARY" ]
Search as the given user. \fITYPE\fR is one of \fBmovie\fR, \fBshow\fR,
\fBepisode\fR, \fBtrack\fR, \fBphoto\fR, \fBalbum\fR, \fBartist\fR, and only
the libraries that can hold that type are searched. A library that cannot be
searched is skipped rather than aborting, and is warned about only when the
search found nothing at all.
.TP
.BI show\-list " ratingKey " [ \-\-m3u " PATH" ]
List every episode of a show, skipping specials.
.TP
.BI show\-metadata " ratingKey"
Show full metadata for one item, with fields varying by media type, including
the server\-side path of every file backing it. A table names a lone file
.BR file ,
and numbers them when there are several; a machine\-readable format carries
them as a
.B files
list. Shows and seasons have no media of their own, so they list none: those
files belong to the episodes.
.TP
.BI titles\-export " library path " [ \-\-sort " ORDER" "] [" \-\-album " ALBUM" ]
Write a whole library to an M3U file, or to a self\-contained HTML gallery for
a photo library. \fIORDER\fR is \fBalpha\fR (default), \fBdate\fR, or
\fBrandom\fR.
.
.SS Playlists
A new playlist is always constructed fully in memory, validated, previewed,
and then created in exactly one API call.
.B \-\-dry\-run
stops after the preview.
.TP
.BI merge\-build " user name first second " [ \-\-ratio " A:B" "] [" \-s "] [" \-U ]
Mix two playlists into one new playlist, taking
.I A
items from the first, then
.I B
from the second, in turn; the default ratio is 1:1. Each keeps its own order,
and once one runs out the rest of the other follows;
.BR \-s ", " \-\-stop\-short
stops there instead.
.BR \-U ", " \-\-unique
skips an item already taken, and the next takes its place. Both are read
before anything is written, so either may also be the destination.
.TP
.BR playlist\-build " (" \-I " | " \-C ") \fIname ratingKey\fR ..."
.TQ
.BI "playlist\-build \-R" " user source dest"
.TQ
.BI "playlist\-build \-A" " user playlist ratingKey ..."
Build a playlist, or add to one. Exactly one of the four flags says how, and
with it what the arguments are.
.RS
.TP
.BR \-I ", " \-\-interleaved
Round\-robin the episodes of several shows into the new playlist
.IR name .
.TP
.BR \-C ", " \-\-chronological
Order shows and movies by original air date. An episode with no date is
estimated from the median interval between its neighbours in the same season,
and you are prompted only when that is impossible. A film with no release date
is prompted for too. Without a terminal to answer on, the command stops naming
the item that needs a date.
.TP
.BR \-R ", " \-\-randomize
Shuffle the playlist
.I source
into the new playlist
.IR dest ,
using
.BR secrets (3)
rather than a predictable generator.
.TP
.BR \-A ", " \-\-append
Append items to the existing playlist
.IR playlist .
Nothing is created, so none of the options below apply, and giving one is an
error. A smart playlist is refused.
.RE
.TP
.BI playlist\-clean " user playlist " [ \-\-include\-partial ]
Remove the watched items from a playlist, leaving the rest in their existing
order. The entries to be dropped are previewed first, numbered by their place
in the playlist as it stands.
.BI \-\-include\-partial
also drops items that are only part\-played; by default a resume point is left
alone, since it is what you are in the middle of watching, and that includes
an item played before and part way through again. A smart playlist is
refused, its contents being defined by a filter rather than by its entries.
.TP
.BI "playlist\-copy \-A" " user playlist " [ \-o ]
.TQ
.BI "playlist\-copy \-T" " user playlist to_user dest " [ \-o ]
Copy a user\(aqs playlist to other accounts. Exactly one of the two flags
says to whom.
.RS
.TP
.BR \-A ", " \-\-all
Copy to every user the server is shared with, skipping the source user. The
item list is printed once, then each user produces a single line giving its
outcome:
\fBcreated\fR, \fBreplaced\fR, \fBskipped\fR, or \fBfailed\fR. A failure for one
user does not end the run. In a machine\-readable format the outcomes are
emitted as one record per user instead.
.TP
.BR \-T ", " \-\-to
Copy to the one user
.IR to_user ,
as the playlist
.IR dest .
.RE
.IP
Without
.BR \-o ", " \-\-overwrite ,
a copy whose name is taken falls back to appending
.RB \(dq " admin copy" \(dq;
if that is taken too the target is skipped with a warning and nothing is
created or deleted.
.TP
.BI playlist\-export " user playlist path"
Write a playlist to an M3U file.
.TP
.BI playlist\-list " user playlist " [ \-\-m3u " PATH" ]
List the items in one playlist, named or by ratingKey.
.TP
.BI playlist\-remove " user playlist"
Delete a playlist.
.TP
.BI playlists\-list " user"
List a user\(aqs playlists.
.PP
.B merge\-build
and
.BR playlist\-build ,
except with
.BR \-A ,
accept
.BR \-o ", " \-\-overwrite ,
.BI \-\-m3u " PATH",
.BR \-p ", " \-\-prefix ,
and the two target options below.
.TP
.BI \-u " USER" \fR,\fP " " \-\-user " USER"
Create the playlist for this user rather than for the account the items came
from. Accepts a user ID or a user title.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-all\-users
Create the playlist for every user on the server, the admin account included.
Mutually exclusive with
.BR \-u .
.PP
With either option the item list is printed once and each user then produces a
single line giving its outcome:
\fBcreated\fR, \fBreplaced\fR, \fBskipped\fR, or \fBfailed\fR. Lines appear as
each user completes, so a long run shows progress. A user the server will not
act for, and a user whose name is already taken, are both reported and the run
carries on; neither ends it. In a machine\-readable format the outcomes are
emitted as one record per user instead of the item list.
.PP
M3U files hold the Plex server\(aqs own filesystem paths, not HTTP URLs.
.BR \-p ", " \-\-prefix
replaces the server\-side library root with another, for reading the export
where the media is mounted differently; separators follow the prefix, so a
Windows prefix produces backslashes. Because the library \fIroot\fR is what is
replaced, one prefix maps every library to the same place: export libraries
separately if they are mounted apart.
.
.SS Playback and watched state
.TP
.BI player\-remote\-play " host ratingKey " [ \-\-port " PORT" "] [" \-\-player\-user " USER" "] [" \-\-server\-url " URL" ]
Connect to a player directly and tell it to start playing an item at once,
replacing whatever it was playing. A
.I host
that is a name is resolved here, and only the address is sent, since some
players refuse a Host header carrying a name as a defence against DNS
rebinding; each address the name has is tried until a player answers on
.BR \-\-port ,
or, without it, on the port the player gives when asked over GDM, or on
the ports the common Plex apps use. The command is sent as
.BR plexdo \(aqs
own account when that is the server\(aqs admin or is signed in to the
player, otherwise as an account from a per\-user configuration section
that is signed in to it, and otherwise as
.BR plexdo \(aqs
own anyway, with a warning.
.B \-\-player\-user
names the account signed in to the player instead, by plex.tv user id,
username, or email, or a managed user\(aqs name; it must be
.BR plexdo \(aqs
own account, a member of its Plex Home, switched into without a password,
or have a per\-user configuration section, and is an error otherwise. The player is told to fetch from whichever of
the server\(aqs addresses is most like its own: the configured URL and every
direct address plex.tv lists are ranked by family, then by private or global
scope, then by the longest shared prefix. Loopback, link\-local, and relayed
addresses never qualify.
.B \-\-server\-url
gives the address outright instead.
.TP
.BI read " ratingKey"
Stream a media file to standard output, for piping into a player or
redirecting to a file. Quitting the player early is not an error.
.RS
.PP
.EX
plexdo read 12345 | mpv \-
plexdo read 12345 > episode.mkv
.EE
.PP
On Linux with a display attached but no desktop environment,
.BR mpv (1)
can draw straight to the console through the kernel modesetting driver, so
playback needs neither X nor Wayland:
.PP
.EX
plexdo read 12345 | mpv \-\-vo=drm \-
.EE
.PP
Run that from a virtual terminal rather than a terminal emulator, and make
sure your user can reach the DRM device, usually by being in the
.B video
group.
.RE
.
.TP
.BI show\-watched\-cp " user_a user_b " [ \-1 "] [" \-l " LIBRARY" "] [" \-t " KEY" "] [" \-\-unwatch ]
Synchronise watched state and resume points between two users.
.RS
.PP
By default the more recent \fBlastViewedAt\fR wins. With
.B \-\-unwatch
an item unwatched for either user becomes unwatched for both, and where both
have progress the earliest \fBlastViewedAt\fR wins instead. Whether an item
has been played and its resume point are both copied, as they stand, so an
item being watched again keeps both; the number of plays is not copied.
.TP
.BR \-1 ", " \-\-one\-way
Only write to the second user; never modify the first.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-library " \fILIBRARY\fR"
Restrict to one library.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-title " \fIKEY\fR"
Restrict to one item, by ratingKey, or to every episode of a show or season,
or every track of an artist or album, each synchronised on its own.
.RE
.IP
Preview a full\-library run with
.B \-\-dry\-run
first: it compares every item both users can see.
.TP
.BI wait " " [ \-s " KEY" "] [" \-r " KEY" "] [" \-a "] [" \-i " SECONDS" "] [" \-\-fast\-interval " SECONDS" "] [" \-\-no\-credits "] [" \-\-offset " SECONDS" "] [" \-\-paused " SECONDS" "] [" \-\-now ]
Sleep until a session\(aqs current play is over, then exit 0. By default the
first session listed is followed and the wait is for whatever it is playing.
.RS
.PP
The wait is over when playback reaches the start of the end credits, if the
video has a credits marker, or its end otherwise. Where a film carries two
credits markers around a mid\-credits scene, the final one is used, so the
scene is not cut off. The wait is also over the moment the video stops
playing: the viewer quit, or autoplay moved on to the next one.
.PP
It polls every 10 seconds, then every second from 30 seconds before the end
point on; each delay varies by up to 20% either way. Every poll prints the
session\(aqs user, what it is playing, the video waited for, the playback
position, the time waited so far, and the estimated time left. In a
machine\-readable format each poll is one record instead: a line of JSON, a
YAML document, or a CSV row under a single header. CLIXML cannot be streamed
and is refused.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-session " \fIKEY\fR"
Follow the session with this sessionKey, from
.BR "status \-\-section sessions" .
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-rating\-key " \fIKEY\fR"
Wait for this video. Without
.BR \-\-session ,
whichever session plays it is followed. If it is not playing, every poll says
so and the wait lasts until it starts.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-all
Wait for every play by every user, each judged by the same rules, with one line
or record per session each poll and a session that disappears reported once
as stopped. The wait is over only when two polls an interval apart both find
nothing left, so a play started in the meantime, an autoplayed next episode
included, is waited for too. Takes neither
.B \-s
nor
.BR \-r .
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-interval " \fISECONDS\fR"
Seconds between polls. Default 10.
.TP
.BI \-\-fast\-interval " SECONDS"
Seconds between polls near the end point. Default 1.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-credits
Ignore the credits marker and wait for the video itself to end.
.TP
.BI \-\-offset " SECONDS"
Negative: end the wait that many seconds before the end point. Positive: sleep
that many seconds once the wait is over.
.TP
.BI \-\-paused " SECONDS"
Count a play as over once its player has sat paused this long. Plex does not
say when a pause began, so the count starts at the first poll that sees it,
and resuming starts it again.
.B 0
ends the play at the first sight of a pause.
.TP
.B \-\-now
With
.BR \-r :
if the video is not playing, exit 0 at once rather than waiting for it.
.RE
.
.SS Server
.TP
.BI rescan " library " [ \-n ]
.TQ
.BR rescan " " \-a " [" \-n ]
.TQ
.BR rescan " " \-s
Trigger a scan of one library for new files, or with
.B \-a
.RB ( \-\-all )
of every library. Naming neither is an error that says to use
.BR \-\-all .
.B \-s
.RB ( \-\-status )
lists the server's background activity instead, library scans among it, and
needs no library; with none, a machine\-readable format gives an empty list.
.B \-n
.RB ( \-\-now )
cancels pending scans on every library first.
.TP
.BI status " " [ \-\-section " SECTION" ]
Report server identity, active sessions (with the sessionKey, library,
ratingKey, and the file the player has open), shared users, system accounts,
reachable addresses, scans in progress, other background activity, and
scheduled tasks. A section that cannot be read is warned about and left empty
rather than losing the whole report. \fB\-\-format csv\fR and
\fB\-\-format clixml\fR are flat and require \fB\-\-section\fR.
.TP
.B users\-list
List users with their ID, account type (\fBmanaged\fR, \fBhome\fR,
\fBfriend\fR, \fBshared\fR), title, username, and email.
.
.SS Account and configuration
.TP
.BI login " " [ \-u " USER" "] [" \-p " PASS" "] [" \-\-save\-password "] [" \-c " CODE" "] [" \-2 ]
Authenticate against plex.tv and save the token in the system keyring.
.B \-\-save\-password
saves the password beside it, so plexdo can sign in again by itself once the
token stops working.
.B \-\-password
is insecure: it is recorded in shell history and is briefly visible to
.BR ps (1).
Prefer the interactive prompt.
.TP
.BI logout " " [ \-u " USER" ]
Sign every saved session out of plex.tv, invalidating its token, and remove
it, with any password saved for the account; with
.BR \-u ,
only the one saved under that name, which must be there. A token plex.tv
already refuses is removed too; one that could not be signed out is kept, and
the exit status is 1.
.TP
.BI set\-pin " USER_ID " [ \-\-remove ]
Prompt for a Plex Home member\(aqs four\-digit PIN and save it in the system
keyring, for
.B player\-remote\-play \-\-player\-user
to switch into the member;
.B \-\-remove
removes it.
.TP
.BI write\-config\-example " " [ \-o ]
Write a template configuration file. An existing one is refused unless
.BR \-o ,
.BR \-\-overwrite
is given, and
.B \-\-dry\-run
writes nothing.
.B \-\-help
prints the exact template that would be written.
.TP
.BI write\-secrets\-example " " [ \-o ]
Write an example secrets file, with a placeholder password and PIN, to the
path
.B plex: secrets_file
names, readable by your account alone. When that option is not set, nothing
is written: standard error says so, and the example is printed to standard
output. An existing file is refused unless
.BR \-o ,
.BR \-\-overwrite
is given, since it may hold real secrets.
.B \-\-help
prints the exact example.
.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Read from
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/plexdo/config.yaml ,
by default
.IR ~/.config/plexdo/config.yaml .
It is YAML, and holds no secret:
.PP
.EX
\-\-\-
plex:
  url: "http://localhost:32400"
  username: "you@example.com"
users:
  99:
    username: "bob@example.com"
.EE
.PP
Under
.BR plex :
.B url
is the server;
.B username
the account
.B plexdo login
signs in as and whose token every command uses;
.B cache_dir
moves the completion cache, and the shell completions honour it too;
.B secret_store
and
.B secrets_file
say where secrets are kept, under SECRETS below.
.B plexdo write\-config\-example
writes the file with every option explained. Values may contain environment
variables as
.B $VAR
or
.BR ${VAR} ,
and
.B ~
for your home directory; a name that is not set is left as literal text and
warned about. An unknown option is warned about by name and ignored, and so is
a password, PIN, or token, which belongs in the keyring.
.PP
An entry under
.B users
is another account plexdo may act as, keyed by its numeric user ID. It is used
only when the server refuses the admin\-issued token for that user, which
happens when nothing has been shared with them, and by
.BR player\-remote\-play .
Sign each in once with
.BR "plexdo login \-\-username" .
.PP
A plexdo 1.x
.I ~/.local/etc/plexdo.ini
is moved across by the first run of any command that finds it and no
.IR config.yaml :
its options into
.IR config.yaml ,
and its passwords and PINs, and the tokens of its token file, into the
keyring. It is then renamed
.I plexdo.ini.migrated
with its secrets removed, and the token file is deleted.
.
.SH SECRETS
Tokens, passwords saved by
.BR "login \-\-save\-password" ,
and PINs saved by
.B set\-pin
are kept in the system keyring: the Secret Service, such as GNOME Keyring or
KWallet, on Linux and BSD; the Keychain on macOS; Credential Manager on
Windows. Each kind is one entry under the service name
.BR plexdo ,
holding a JSON object; tokens are filed by username:
.PP
.EX
{
  "@admin": "xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx",
  "bob@example.com": "yyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyy"
}
.EE
.PP
.B @admin
is the reserved key used for a token saved with no username known;
.B @
cannot occur in a Plex username, so it never collides.
.PP
Where there is no keyring, as on a headless machine, secrets go in the file
.B plex: secrets_file
names, by default
.IR $XDG_STATE_HOME/plexdo/secrets.json ,
created readable by its owner alone, with a warning that it is not encrypted.
.B plex: secret_store
set to
.B file
uses that file always, for a machine whose keyring is locked when plexdo runs,
as under cron, and silences the warning; set to
.BR keyring ,
the keyring is insisted on.
.PP
With
.B plex: secret_store
set to
.BR file ,
only saved passwords and PINs go in that file. Tokens are sessions, and are
kept apart from them, in
.IR $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/plexdo/session.json ,
or, where there is no runtime directory, in a directory of your own under the
temporary directory.
.B plexdo login
says on standard error where the session went. It does not survive a reboot:
run
.B plexdo login
again then, which asks for nothing when the password was saved. Tokens left in
the secrets file by an earlier version are moved to the session file once.
.B plexdo write\-secrets\-example
writes an example of the file, or prints one when
.B plex: secrets_file
is not set.
.PP
When a command meets a 401 for a user, three sources are tried in turn: the
server\-scoped token the admin can mint, a token already saved, and a fresh
login with a password saved for that user\(aqs
.B users
entry. A token obtained by the last is saved, so the login happens once rather
than on every run.
.B plexdo logout
signs the tokens out and removes them.
.
.SH SHELL COMPLETION
Completions are provided for
.BR bash ,
.BR zsh ,
.BR fish ,
and
.BR pwsh ,
covering user and library IDs and titles, rating keys, playlist names and
keys, and photo album names. Numeric IDs are shown with the title they belong
to.
.PP
Values are read from a 15\-minute cache, in
.B plex: cache_dir
or else
.IR $XDG_CACHE_HOME/plexdo ,
by default
.IR ~/.cache/plexdo ,
populated as a side effect of the list commands. A stale cache is refreshed in
the background, so completion never blocks; the values offered are whatever is
cached at that moment. Playlists and photo album names are cached under every
spelling of their user or library, the ID, the title, and the word as typed,
so they complete whichever of those is on the command line.
.PP
.B make install
installs all three. See
.B make help
for the paths in use.
.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Failure: missing configuration or token, an unknown ID, an inaccessible user,
a name collision refused without \fB\-\-overwrite\fR, or an unsupported
combination of options.
.TP
.B 2
The command line could not be understood: an unknown command or option, a
missing argument, or a value of the wrong kind.
.TP
.B 130
.B wait
was interrupted before the play was over, so a command chained after it with
.B &&
does not run.
.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
The parent of the configuration directory on Linux, BSD, and macOS, when an
absolute path; otherwise
.IR ~/.config .
.TP
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
The parent of the secrets file\(aqs directory, when an absolute path; otherwise
.IR ~/.local/state .
.TP
.B XDG_CACHE_HOME
The parent of the completion cache, when an absolute path; otherwise
.IR ~/.cache .
.TP
.B XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
The parent of the session file\(aqs directory, when an absolute path and
.B plex: secret_store
is
.BR file .
Otherwise the temporary directory is used, which
.BR TMPDIR ,
.BR TEMP ,
and
.B TMP
name.
.TP
.B LOCALAPPDATA
On Windows, the parent of the configuration and cache directories.
.B APPDATA
is used when it is unset.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Not used; output is never coloured.
.
.SH FILES
Paths follow the platform. On Linux, BSD, and macOS, by default:
.TP
.I ~/.config/plexdo/config.yaml
Configuration.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/plexdo/secrets.json
Secrets, only where the system keyring is not used; mode 0600.
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/plexdo/session.json
Session tokens, only when
.B plex: secret_store
is
.BR file ;
mode 0600, in a directory of mode 0700.
.TP
.I ~/.cache/plexdo/
Completion cache.
.PP
On Windows:
.TP
.I %LOCALAPPDATA%\ePlexDo\econfig.yaml
Configuration.
.TP
.I %LOCALAPPDATA%\ePlexDo\esecrets.json
Secrets, only where Credential Manager is not used.
.TP
.I %LOCALAPPDATA%\ePlexDo\eCache
Completion cache.
.
.SH EXAMPLES
Create the configuration and authenticate:
.PP
.EX
plexdo write\-config\-example
plexdo login
.EE
.PP
List libraries, then the titles in one of them by name:
.PP
.EX
plexdo libraries\-list
plexdo library\-list "TV Shows"
.EE
.PP
Build a playlist interleaving two shows, replacing any existing one:
.PP
.EX
plexdo playlist\-build \-I "Mixed Run" 101 202 \-\-overwrite
.EE
.PP
Preview a watched\-state sync before running it for real:
.PP
.EX
plexdo \-\-dry\-run show\-watched\-cp Alice Bob
plexdo show\-watched\-cp Alice Bob \-\-one\-way
.EE
.PP
Export a library for a client that mounts the media elsewhere:
.PP
.EX
plexdo titles\-export "TV Shows" ~/tv.m3u \-\-prefix \(aq\e\eNAS\emedia\(aq
.EE
.PP
Stream an episode into a player:
.PP
.EX
plexdo read 12345 | mpv \-
.EE
.PP
Restart the server once the current episode reaches its credits:
.PP
.EX
plexdo wait && sudo systemctl restart plexmediaserver
.EE
.PP
Start a film on the living room TV, by its name on the local network:
.PP
.EX
plexdo player\-remote\-play livingroom\-tv.lan 881
.EE
.PP
Sign out of plex.tv and remove every saved token:
.PP
.EX
plexdo logout
.EE
.PP
Machine\-readable output:
.PP
.EX
plexdo \-\-json users\-list | jq \(aq.[] | select(.type == "home")\(aq
plexdo status \-\-section sessions \-f csv
.EE
.
.SH NOTES
On Windows,
.B \-\-password
scrubbing is best effort: the technique that hides the value from
.BR ps (1)
has no Windows equivalent, so it stays visible in the process list.
.
